  "worked_solution": "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{\u2032}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/bff5cf9d21f0b01571b577ebe3d98ef08525d13a70a1d7379b537f6ba5319613.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4t+3 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/7d590ee38f7f1b6b43c9b650890f42fa31b4c15951dab8bcb003b1ac1cc45c39.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4t-3 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/b965e3e244bc49b5e0f52840550c2ae997a0e38dc40e8757f0f51905c448d7af.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png)",
